  "grammar_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Talking About the Past ('rakamira')",
      "content": "The verb 'rakamira' expresses a completed past action. Shona has two main ways to talk about the past: 1) For actions that happened earlier today or very recently, combine the short subject prefix ('nda-' = I, 'wa-' = you, 'a-' = he/she, 'ta-' = we, 'va-' = they) directly with the verb stem (e.g., 'ndamuka' = 'I woke up today', 'wabudirira' = 'you have succeeded'). 2) For events that happened yesterday or further in the past, insert '-ka-' after the subject prefix (e.g., 'takatenga' = 'we bought', 'vakaenda' = 'they went', 'akadanidzira' = 'he shouted').",
      "rule": "Recent Today Past: [nda- / wa- / ta- / va-] + verb | Earlier Past: [ndaka- / waka- / taka- / vaka-] + verb"
    },
    {
      "title": "Actions Happening Right Now: The '-ri ku-' Progressive",
      "content": "To describe an action that is happening right now ('I am doing', 'it is raining', 'they are walking'), Shona uses the helper verb '-ri' ('am/is/are') followed by 'ku-' ('to') and the action verb. For people, the forms are 'ndiri ku-' (I am), 'uri ku-' (you are), 'ari ku-' (he/she is), 'tiri ku-' (we are), and 'vari ku-' (they are). In fast speech or writing, the two words are sometimes joined together (e.g., 'ndiri kuenda' or 'ndirikuenda').",
      "rule": "[Subject + -ri] + ku-[verb] -> Ndiri kuverenga (I am reading) | Vari kufamba (They are walking) | Mvura iri kunaya (It is raining)"
    },
    {
      "title": "Present Tense & Everyday Actions ('Rinoratidza')",
      "content": "The verb 'Rinoratidza' uses the present marker '-no-'. In Shona, inserting '-no-' between the subject prefix and the verb stem expresses general truths, everyday habits, or states of mind like 'wanting', 'thinking', and 'knowing'. For example: 'ndinoda' ('I want/like'), 'unofunga' ('you think'), 'vanoti' ('they say'), and 'vanorima' ('they farm').",
      "rule": "[ndi- (I) / u- (you) / a- (he/she) / ti- (we) / va- (they)] + -no- + verb -> Ndinoda (I want), Vanogara (They live)"
    }
  ],
  "cultural_notes": [
    {
      "title": "Ancestral Roots ('Kumusha') & Great Zimbabwe Heritage",
      "content": "Even for families who live and work in cities like Harare, the rural family homestead ('kumusha')—where grandparents ('mbuya' and 'sekuru') often live—remains the spiritual and cultural heart of the family. Travelling 'kumusha' during holidays strengthens family bonds. On a national scale, the dry-stone walls of Great Zimbabwe ('Masvingo eGreat Zimbabwe'), built centuries ago without mortar, gave modern Zimbabwe its name ('Dzimba dzemabwe' — Houses of Stone).",
      "rule": null
    }
  ],
